WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.842
The product totals CTE creates one row per department and product.

00:00:04.842 --> 00:00:11.012
The ranked CTE keeps the same grain but adds DENSE_RANK within each department.

00:00:11.012 --> 00:00:13.632
The final SELECT filters to the top two rank groups.

00:00:13.632 --> 00:00:19.277
Run the query and trace the grain through all three stages.

00:00:19.277 --> 00:00:23.007
This is a strong example of readable advanced SQL.

00:00:23.007 --> 00:00:28.107
When reading the example, do not read punctuation one character at a time.

00:00:28.107 --> 00:00:32.427
Start with the purpose of the query, then identify the input rows, the operation being applied,

00:00:32.427 --> 00:00:36.792
and the shape of the expected output.

00:00:36.792 --> 00:00:41.757
In a real setting such as a hospital ranking visits and identifying readmissions,

00:00:41.757 --> 00:00:47.792
this habit makes code reviews much easier because you can explain what the query is supposed to guarantee

00:00:47.792 --> 00:00:50.112
before discussing syntax.

00:00:50.112 --> 00:00:56.400
After running it, inspect a few records manually and check that the result matches that explanation.
